# Public `swage.segment_reduce` on RTX A6000 at `2cf88ae`

This record times the public `swage.segment_reduce` call on one {{gpu}}
(`{{target}}`) at revision `{{revision}}`, with a new offsets layout on every
call, beside `torch.segment_reduce` and looped Triton kernels. It holds
five runs, each repeated in five independent processes: one run of
rank-one values and four runs of `[N, D]` values.

It repeats the runs of the
[`{{baseline_revision}}` record](segment-reduce-a6000-sm86-c6099ec.md) with
the same commands, after two changes: `[N, D]` values run the row-stripe
tile of ADR-0023, and the segmented calls record gradients (ADR-0024). The
harness files did not change between the two revisions. Its values do not
require grad, so no timed call records a gradient. Every `[N, D]` row here
runs the kernels that
[The row-stripe kernels at this revision](#the-row-stripe-kernels-at-this-revision)
describes. [Against the baseline](#against-the-baseline) compares the two
records.

These are research measurements, not a performance gate. The machine was not
idle while they were taken; [Machine and conditions](#machine-and-conditions)
says what ran.

The record names revision `2cf88ae`. The branch history was rewritten after
the run, to replace the same scratch path in the process records of the
`c6099ec` record, so that commit is not in the published history. Its
counterpart is `65235ee`: the two trees differ only in the process records
and summaries of the `c6099ec` record directory, which the harness does not
read.

## What the record holds

The directory
[`segment-reduce-a6000-sm86-2cf88ae/`](segment-reduce-a6000-sm86-2cf88ae/)
holds five runs:

{{runs_table}}

Each run directory holds:

- `summary.json`, written by `benchmarks/benchmark_processes.py`: for every
  row, timing method, and candidate, the median of each process and the
  median, minimum, and maximum of those five medians. It is committed as the
  driver wrote it, except for the SHA-256 of each process record, which the
  next item describes.
- `process-1.json.xz` to `process-5.json.xz`: the record of each process
  with every raw sample, compressed with `xz -9`. Before the record was
  committed, the output path that each process record stores was replaced
  by `<work>`, because it named a scratch directory of the machine. Each
  decompressed file has the SHA-256 that `summary.json` lists for it under
  `process_records`; those digests were updated to match.

The record directory also holds:

- [`conditions.txt`](segment-reduce-a6000-sm86-2cf88ae/conditions.txt): the
  machine state sampled at the start and at the end of the campaign.
- `segmented_sum_r2.cta.ptx`, `segmented_sum_r2.partial.ptx`, and
  `segmented_sum_r2.merge.ptx`: the PTX of the three row-stripe kernels that
  the processes loaded.
- [`page.md.in`](segment-reduce-a6000-sm86-2cf88ae/page.md.in): the template
  of this page.

To read a process record, or to summarize a run against other reference
candidates with `benchmark_processes.py --summarize`, decompress it first:

```sh
cd benchmarks/results/segment-reduce-a6000-sm86-2cf88ae/public-r2-2048
xz -dk process-*.json.xz
```

`xz -dk` keeps the compressed files. Do not commit the decompressed ones.

